"reasoning": "The patient presents with a specific constellation of symptoms including recurrent headaches, distorted eyesight, excessive appetite, painful neck, mood changes (melancholy, irritability), and gastrointestinal issues (acid reflux, indigestion). While acid reflux and indigestion might suggest a gastrointestinal condition like GERD or peptic ulcer disease, they do not account for the visual distortions, neck pain, or mood alterations. Similarly, cervical spondylosis explains neck pain but not the visual, mood, or GI symptoms. This exact combination of neurological, visual, mood, and prodromal GI symptoms strongly aligns with the clinical presentation of a migraine, which frequently includes aura (visual disturbances), neck stiffness, and emotional changes. Reference [1] explicitly maps this identical symptom profile to migraine.",
"final_answer": "[DIAGNOSIS]migraine[/DIAGNOSIS]"
